In article <1991May4.025731.29253@colorado.edu> bear@tigger.Colorado.EDU (Bear Giles) writes:
)While messing around with zapping myself, I found two possible bugs:
)
)1.  A wand of cancellation will erase all scrolls, and "purify" potions, 
)    but it does not erase spellbooks, wands, or rings.  Shouldn't it?

	It does have an effect on wands and rings: wands will lose all their
charges, and rings that have a number associated (such as "Gain Strength"
or "Protection") will be turned into +0 rings.

	Incidentally, it's interesting to note that potions of fruit juice
are intrinsically non-magical, and are not turned to water by a wand of
cancellation.

)2.  A wand of digging does not kill me.  I would expect to see blood and
)    guts and bone fragments and general, all-around gore from anything
)    which can punch a hole in that much rock, but.... nothing.

	Think of it as a "Stone-to-mud" spell. You are not made out of stone,
so there is no effect. It does seem that gemstones ought to be destroyed, but
then wands of digging do not do anything to boulders.

	This does suggest the refinement that a wand of digging ought to
do large amounts of damage to earth-based creatures: e.g. xorns, stone giants,
and earth elementals. This would make them slightly more useful. Moria 
already has this with its "Stone-to-mud" spell.

)Bear Giles			

